<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for TwinVPN.app — the containing app.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.9's macOS row, verbatim, for this component:

    "App: App Sandbox on, `com.apple.security.network.client`,
     `com.apple.developer.system-extension.install`."

  and §11.2's macOS component row: "TwinVPN.app (per-user, sandboxed)".

  ============================================= WHAT THE APP IS NOT ALLOWED TO BE

  ADR-0016 §11.2's "macOS, normatively" makes the system extension the
  authority. The app is a client: it presents UI, it installs the VPN profile,
  and it activates the extension. It holds NO enforcement authority, NO key, and
  NO recovery path.

  That last one is a rule, not a description. FC-1 §6 instance 5 puts the
  SIGNED-CONTRACT FETCH in the EXTENSION and core-lite's parse and verification
  behind it, precisely so that a device whose app is not running — or is
  uninstalled — can still recover. An app that could fetch the contract would
  become a dependency of recovery, and the fail-closed ladder would then have a
  rung that requires a GUI.

  `com.apple.security.network.client` below therefore exists for the app's own
  update check and telemetry submission, NOT for the contract. Nothing in the
  app's code path fetches a contract, and if that ever changes, FC-1 is what it
  breaks.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- §11.9: "App Sandbox on". The app parses untrusted presentation data and
         renders it; §6's option-A analysis is explicit that font, image, markup
         and URL parsing must not run with privilege. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      §11.9: `com.apple.developer.system-extension.install`.

      Without it, OSSystemExtensionRequest.activationRequest fails and the
      extension can never be installed. It is what makes
      SystemExtensionInstaller.swift work at all.

      This entitlement is granted by Apple as part of the same application as
      the NetworkExtension one (ADR-0016 P-06); it is not separately obtainable.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.system-extension.install</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      §11.9: `com.apple.security.network.client`.

      For the app's OWN outbound requests only — see the header. A sandboxed app
      has no outbound network at all without it, so its absence would break the
      update check silently rather than loudly.

      `com.apple.security.network.server` is deliberately ABSENT: the app listens
      for nothing. An app that could listen would be a second local management
      surface, and ADR-0017 MI-20 allows one contract with two carriages, never
      two contracts.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Installing and managing the VPN profile. ADR-0016 §11.2's iOS capability
      table assigns "NETunnelProviderManager configuration and VPN-profile
      installation" to the APP, because "only the app can present the consent
      flow", and macOS is the same in this respect.

      UNVERIFIED whether the macOS system-extension shape needs
      `com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api` on the app as well as (or instead
      of) on the extension. Both bundles carry it here; see the note in
      TwinVPNTunnel.entitlements. One of the two is probably unnecessary and an
      unnecessary entitlement is still surface, so this is a thing to delete
      once a Mac build says which.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api</key>
    <array>
        <string>allow-vpn</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Paired with the extension's. See the long note in
         TwinVPNTunnel.entitlements: this group deliberately does NOT hold the
         identity key, which lives in the System keychain with an ACL bound to
         the Team-signed binary (ADR-0020's macOS Developer-ID row), because a
         shared access group cannot be scoped below app identity (ADR-0016
         §11.4's residual). -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.twinvpn.shared</string>
    </array>

    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)com.twinvpn.shared</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      Sandbox file access. The app reads the diagnostic ring buffer out of the
      app-group container and writes a bundle where the user chooses, which is
      what `files.user-selected.read-write` is for: ADR-0015 requires local user
      authorization for a diagnostic bundle anyway, and a save panel IS that
      authorization rather than a step in front of it.

      There is NO `files.all` and no temporary-exception entitlement. The app has
      no business reading /Library/Application Support/TwinVPN/ — that is the
      authority's store root at 0700 root:wheel, and the app being able to read
      it would defeat ADR-0016 §11.5's "unopenable by the unprivileged client".
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      DELIBERATELY ABSENT:
        com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        com.apple.security.get-task-allow
        com.apple.security.files.all / temporary-exception.*
        com.apple.security.device.* (no camera, no microphone, no USB)
      Each for the reason given in TwinVPNTunnel.entitlements, and each checked
      by the `codesign` entitlements dump of SIGNING.md §4 rather than by review.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
